## Context

The bean IDs `querystore.bridge.indexer` and `querystore.bridge.dispatcher` are part of querystore's de-facto public SPI surface — every contributing module that writes a custom advice needs to look up these beans. But the constants and accessor methods that internally resolve them live in `AbstractIndexingAdvice` with **package-private** visibility:

```java
// querystore-side: AbstractIndexingAdvice.java
BridgeIndexer indexer() {
    return Context.getRegisteredComponent("querystore.bridge.indexer", BridgeIndexer.class);
}

AfterCommitDispatcher dispatcher() {
    return Context.getRegisteredComponent("querystore.bridge.dispatcher", AfterCommitDispatcher.class);
}
```

A custom advice in a contributing module (e.g. my appointments-side `RecurringAppointmentIndexingAdvice` and `AppointmentProviderResponseIndexingAdvice` from #19) lives in a different package, so it can't call these methods. It also can't reference the bean-ID string constants — they're not exposed.

## Concrete impact

My slice ended up with two parallel constants in `RecurringAppointmentIndexingAdvice`:

```java
static final String BRIDGE_INDEXER_BEAN_ID = "querystore.bridge.indexer";
static final String BRIDGE_DISPATCHER_BEAN_ID = "querystore.bridge.dispatcher";
```

`AppointmentProviderResponseIndexingAdvice` reuses them by fully-qualified reference. That's a hardcoded string drift hazard: if querystore renames `querystore.bridge.indexer` to `querystore.bridge.indexerV2`, the contributing-module copies fall out of sync silently — `Context.getRegisteredComponent(...)` returns null, the advice's outer catch swallows, and every appointment write quietly bypasses querystore. The `<require_module>` declaration without a version pin doesn't catch this kind of drift.

## Suggested fix

Two options, either of which closes the magic-string drift gap:

1. **Public string constants on a stable class** (e.g. `BridgeBeans.INDEXER_BEAN_ID`, `BridgeBeans.DISPATCHER_BEAN_ID`). Contributing modules import the constants. A rename surfaces at compile time across every contributor when they next pull the upstream version.

2. **Promote `AbstractIndexingAdvice.indexer()` and `dispatcher()` to `protected`** so subclasses (in any package, since Java's `protected` honors inheritance across packages) can call them directly. Custom advices not extending the base would still need the constants per option 1 — so these are complementary, not alternatives.

## Backwards compatibility

Both options are additive. Promoting visibility from package-private to protected is binary-compatible. Adding new public constants is binary-compatible.

## Related

Part of the same "make `AbstractIndexingAdvice` extensible for contributing modules" theme as #19. Could be closed in the same upstream change.

## Context

Querystore's own [`ProviderEndToEndTest`](https://github.com/openmrs/openmrs-module-querystore/blob/main/api/src/test/java/org/openmrs/module/querystore/bootstrap/ProviderEndToEndTest.java) builds a synthetic `billing_bill` provider and asserts the full pipeline (discovery → bootstrap → AOP fire → document in index → patient-scoped search → cross-type wildcard search → patient-cascade delete). It verifies that **the SPI contract works generically**.

But a contributing module — appointments, in my case — can't easily run an analogous test against **its specific provider**. The contract-level test in querystore proves the pipeline works for a stand-in provider; it doesn't prove that my `AppointmentResourceProvider` + `AppointmentSerializer` + `AppointmentBootstrapper` + 3 advices work together when the appointments module's Spring context is wired alongside querystore's.

## Concrete impact

In the appointments contribution, the slice has 248 unit tests + 41 IT + 73 omod IT, all green. **None of them** exercise the actual `AppointmentsService.validateAndSave(...) → querystore_appointments_appointment document indexed` pipeline with both modules wired in a real Spring context. The unit tests mock the indexer; the existing IT tests use a `TestingApplicationContext` that doesn't load querystore.

A consumer-visible failure mode I currently can't catch in CI: a typo in the `<advice><point>` reference in `omod/.../config.xml` would silently disable the advice. OpenMRS's module loader doesn't validate the pointcut, the unit tests mock the advice's internals, and no integration test would catch the wire-up break before deployment.

I deferred this in my ADR with: *"belongs in a distribution-level test suite, not in either contributing module"* — but that's a cop-out. The right answer is upstream test infrastructure that contributing modules can use.

## Suggested fix

Ship a `querystore-api-tests` jar (or test-jar classifier on `querystore-api`) with reusable fixtures:

```java
public abstract class QuerystoreProviderTestSupport extends BaseModuleWebContextSensitiveTest {
    // Boots querystore + the contributing module in a real Spring context.
    // Picks an in-memory backend (Lucene per qsADR-3 default).
    // Exposes assertions like:
    protected void assertDocumentExistsFor(String resourceType, String resourceUuid) { ... }
    protected void assertPatientHasDocument(String resourceType, String patientUuid) { ... }
    protected void assertCrossTypeSearchReturns(String query, String... expectedUuids) { ... }
}
```

Contributing modules write a single integration test extending this:

```java
public class AppointmentProviderEndToEndIT extends QuerystoreProviderTestSupport {
    @Test public void savedAppointmentLandsInIndex() {
        Appointment a = ...;
        appointmentsService.validateAndSave(a);
        assertDocumentExistsFor(\"appointments_appointment\", a.getUuid());
    }
}
```

The infrastructure cost (Spring context wiring, in-memory backend setup) is paid once upstream; every contributor benefits.

## Empirical evidence

My ADR documents this gap explicitly as an Open Question (\"Cross-module end-to-end test\") with the reasoning that the test belongs in a distribution suite. Without upstream test support, every contributor either (a) skips the e2e test and relies on smoke-testing, or (b) re-invents the wiring infrastructure module-by-module. Option (a) leaks bugs to first-deployment; option (b) costs every contributor 4–6 hours and produces inconsistent test surfaces.

## Backwards compatibility

Fully additive — a new test-jar artifact doesn't change any existing runtime contract.

## Related

This is the broader infrastructure version of [Decision 13](https://github.com/openmrs/openmrs-module-querystore/blob/main/docs/adr.md#decision-13-module-extension-spi-service-provider-interface-for-custom-resource-types)'s \"Worked example for provider authors.\" The walkthrough names `ProviderEndToEndTest` as the verification harness — but it's a private querystore-side test, not a reusable contributor-side test base.
